Method: worked in a `cp -a /tmp/kd1-scratch` copy for probe tests (deleted
afterward); real tree never modified; final commands re-run against the real
tree read-only (go build/test only, package-scoped, no server run).

## Attacks that did NOT refute the claim

1. Divisor drift via JS query params: handleKPIDetail's default (no
   start/end) falls back to data.MinDate()/MaxDate() (all-time) while
   handleDashboard's resolveDateRange defaults to YTD-clipped. These ARE
   different defaults, but web/templates/pages/dashboard.html:48-49 always
   pre-populates the #date-filter-form inputs with the server-resolved
   {{.StartDate}}/{{.EndDate}}, and dashboard.js's openKPIDetail() reads
   those form values (not blank) before firing the request — so in the
   reachable flow the modal always receives the SAME concrete dates the
   card used. This is pre-existing plumbing shared by all six kinds, not
   KD1-introduced, and not defeated by any reachable state I found.

2. Extraction correctness: `git diff` on handleDashboard's body shows a pure
   read of gatherDashboardCalcInputs replacing the inline
   BudgetTargets/HealthcareCoverageStart/planSyncExclusions sequence with
   identical field-for-field values; go test ./internal/handlers/dashboard/
   ./internal/services/metrics/ green (ok, 1.285s / 0.006s) on the real
   tree.

3. Refund-heavy-month Abs shape / rows summing to more than Total: built a
   2-month living fixture (Jan +2000 forced-outflow refund via "Autopay
   Reversal" keyword, Feb Rent -500) in the scratch copy.
   TestKD1Probe_RefundDominantMonthRowsVsTotal showed sumRows=2500,
   total=2500 — they match, because the modal's "Total" field (handlers.go
   line ~605, `"Total": sum`) is DEFINED as the sum of the already-Abs'd
   per-month row values, not metrics.LivingExpensesTotal(range) — so
   "rows sum to Total" is tautological, not a live contradiction. This sum/
   min/max/avg-scaffold loop (lines ~559-611) is 100% pre-existing code,
   untouched by the KD1 diff (only the `case "living"/"healthcare": value =
   ...` lines feed it new inputs) — reused shape on new data, not new
   arithmetic; explicitly directed by spec §Design point 3 ("same
   refund-netting shape the existing code uses") and identical to the
   pre-existing "expenses"/"income" cases immediately above in the same
   switch. Per the calibration rule, not FAIL grounds. Backlog-worthy
   observation only (a refund-dominant month renders as a large positive
   figure with no refund indicator — true today for "expenses" too).

4. vs-Avg divide-by-zero: templates/render.go:822 percentDiff() guards
   reference==0 -> returns 0 (pre-existing, unmodified). No NaN/Inf
   possible from the new kinds' zero-avg case.

5. K6 grep gate: `grep -n "Health Insurance" internal/handlers/dashboard/handlers.go`
   -> 0 matches, confirmed independently.

6. kpis.html / kpi-detail.html diffs: `git diff` shows exactly the claimed
   two-line onclick swaps and the two claimed template edits (header label
   + `.Value` instead of `.Expenses`); for kpiType=="expenses", Value==
   Expenses by construction (case "expenses": value = expAmt, same var the
   old template printed), so existing-kind rendering is unaffected.

## Attack that DID refute the claim

7. Healthcare hasCoverage=false / coverageStart-after-range-end producing a
   real UI contradiction ("two kinds of totals" — the exact thing user
   ruling KD-2026-08-30a vetoed). Built in the scratch copy:
   fixture = [{"2025-01-15","Health Insurance Autopay Reversal","150",
   "Health Insurance"}, {"2025-01-05","Rent","-500","Housing"}]
   ("Autopay" is a documented never-income keyword, forcing Outflow type
   despite the positive amount — verified via the worker's own K2 fixture
   using the identical "Autopay Reversal" pattern for a living refund).
   GET /dashboard/kpi/healthcare?start=2025-01-01&end=2025-01-31 rendered:
     Total: $150.00, Low: $150.00, High: $150.00, Per Month: $0.00
     row 2025-01: Healthcare $150.00, vs Avg 0.0% (styled red/"over")
   Root cause: `monthlyHealthcareOutflows` (row values) is built from
   `outflows.FilterByCategory(HealthInsuranceCategory)` — category-only,
   sign-agnostic. `classifiedCardPerMonth` for healthcare is
   `cardMetrics.HealthcareActual`, which is 0 whenever
   ClippedHealthcareMonths()==0 — which happens whenever hasCoverage is
   false (metrics.HealthcareCoverageStart requires a NEGATIVE-amount HI
   row somewhere in the FULL active set; an all-positive/refund-only HI
   history, or a range that starts before the first real premium, both
   hit this) or coverageStart is after the segment end. These two
   quantities are gated by DIFFERENT signals (category membership vs.
   real-premium coverage), and KD1 is what puts them side by side in one
   modal for the first time — before this task, HealthcareActual only
   ever appeared alone on the dashboard card, so this divergence was never
   visible. This is NEW arithmetic coupling (the avg-override added at
   handlers.go's `if kpiType == "living" || kpiType == "healthcare" { avg
   = classifiedCardPerMonth }`), not a rendering of pre-existing shape, and
   it reproduces exactly the "zero Per Month next to non-zero rows"
   scenario the task brief itself flagged as the two-kinds-of-totals
   confusion the user vetoed. K3's own test only exercises the
   "coverageStart inside the range" happy path, so this gap is untested by
   the manifest's own suite. Confined to "healthcare"; "living"'s divisor
   (MonthsBetween, never zero per TestMonthsBetween_ClampsNegativeRangeTo
   OneDay) cannot reproduce this — living's Per Month can only be zero
   when livingTotal is also zero, which keeps rows and Per Month
   consistent.

## Scope note
This is inside KD1's written scope: the avg-override and the row/Per-Month
juxtaposition are both new code added by this task in
internal/handlers/dashboard/handlers.go (handleKPIDetail), and the
contradiction is only observable through the new "healthcare" kind KD1
introduces.
